<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adrian von Bubenberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1424–<a href="/years/1479">1479</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adrian von Bubenberg was a Bernese knight and statesman, several times Schultheiss (mayor) of Bern. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundian-wars">Burgundian Wars</a> he defended the town of Murten against the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a> in 1476, holding out until the Swiss confederates won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-morat">Battle of Murten</a>. He is remembered as a Swiss national hero.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adrian-von-bubenberg.jpg" alt="Adrian von Bubenberg" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diebold Schilling the Elder (c. 1445-1485) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adrian_von_Bubenberg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Adrian von Bubenberg was born in Bern, the son of Heinrich IV. von Bubenberg, Schultheiss of Bern and lord of Spiez, whom he succeeded in 1465. On a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1466 he was dubbed a Knight of the Holy Sepulchre and inscribed his name and emblem on the wall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>'s Tomb. He headed Bern's leading noble house, yet was always short of money.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Bubenberg was Schultheiss of Bern in 1468 to 1469, 1473 to 1474 and 1477 to 1479. Before and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> wars he often led Bern's negotiations with the other cantons of the Old Swiss Confederacy and with foreign powers, among them Savoy, Burgundy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><p>His place in Swiss history rests on the defence of Murten. He was appointed commander of its garrison in April 1476. In June the troops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles, Duke of Burgundy</a>, besieged the town for twelve days, and Bubenberg held out until confederate forces relieved it on 22 June, in the battle that ended in Charles's decisive defeat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bubenberg died in Bern in August 1479 and was buried in the choir of the Berner Münster. In 1481 he was banned after his death for his debts, but the government resisted the demand of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a> that he be exhumed and given a dishonourable burial.</p><p>His deeds already appear in the illustrated chronicles of Diebold Schilling the Elder. Romantic nationalism made him a model of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knighthood">knightly</a> virtue and patriotism, as in the novel Ring i der Chetti by Rudolf von Tavel (1931). In 1897 Bern put up a bronze statue of him on Christoffelplatz, renamed Bubenbergplatz, after a dispute over whether he should be shown on foot or on horseback, and struck a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> medal in his memory. A popular vote of 6 April 1930 approved moving the statue to the nearby Hirschengraben to make room for tram lines, where it was set up on 19 May.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Adrian von Bubenberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It led the Swiss against Burgundy, with <b>Adrian von Bubenberg</b> holding Murten in 1476.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-bern">Canton of Bern</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bern, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. Died in Bern, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.95,7.45&amp;year=1459" aria-label="Bern on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Adrian von Bubenberg"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5434 6082h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5434 6082h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.95,7.45&amp;year=1459">What was here in 1459? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
